# Code review (/code-review)

When Spencer says `code review` or `/code-review`, read and follow:

`~/.cursor/skills/multi-model-code-review/SKILL.md`

The skill sets the flow, the verification, and the reply format. Reply with
its final reply only.

A skip, xfail, or filter wider than its own reason text, or that un-gates
a test that fails the job on origin/main, is removed behavior. Report it.
Analogizing to a different pre-existing skip or xfail is not a disproof.

Split verdict: re-derive from sources. If the trace names a ship surface,
keep the finding.

No preferences or style nits.

When he pushes back on a finding: re-derive from sources. Keep it if still
blocking; drop it if the path is not real. No apology paragraph.

# Reporting a change

Three bullets, maximum:

1. WHAT changed, one line.
2. WHY, one line.
3. RISKIEST part: the single place this could bite us, and what to check.

If there is no real risk, say "low risk" and stop. Always give blast radius.

# Animated flow diagrams

When a change moves data or control through the system, offer a self-contained
animated SVG: one node per component or step, arrows labelled with what passes,
the sequence animated with SMIL `<animate>` so it plays on open with no
JavaScript, and a short caption of the overall flow at the bottom.

# Decision log

For a meaningful change, draft one or two lines on why it went this way, taken
from the summary above, so it can be explained six months later. Offer it for
the commit body or the ticket folder rather than writing it somewhere unasked.
